.\" GPL-2.0-only
.\" mtr-rs: man page for the privileged probe helper.
.TH MTR-RS-PACKET 8 "2026-09-04" "mtr-rs" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
mtr-rs-packet \- send network probes for mtr-rs and report the replies
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mtr-rs-packet
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mtr-rs-packet
is the privileged half of
.BR mtr-rs (8).
The unprivileged client starts it, writes one command per line on its
standard input and reads one reply per line from its standard output.
It opens its raw sockets first, then drops setgid, setuid and its
capabilities (see
.BR PRIVILEGES )
before serving the first command. It exits when standard input is closed
and no probe is outstanding.
.PP
The line protocol is that of mtr 0.96. A request is
.IR "token command [key value]..." ,
where
.I token
is an integer echoed back in the reply. The commands are
.B check-support
(with
.BR "feature version" ", " ip-4 ", " ip-6 ", " send-probe ", " icmp ", " udp ", " tcp ", " sctp " or " mark ;
the reply is
.BR "feature-support support ok" ,
.BR no ,
or the version string) and
.B send-probe
(with
.BR "ip-4 ADDR" " or " "ip-6 ADDR" ,
and optionally
.BR "protocol icmp|udp|tcp|sctp" ", " "port N" ", " "local-port N" ", "
.BR "local-ip ADDR" ", " "local-device NAME" ", " "ttl N" ", " "size N" ", "
.BR "bit-pattern N" ", " "tos N" ", " "timeout SECONDS" ", " "mark N" ).
.PP
Probe replies are
.BR reply ", " ttl-expired " or " no-route-host ,
each followed by
.BR "ip-4|ip-6 ADDR round-trip-time MICROSECONDS" .
A router that attached RFC 4950 MPLS objects adds
.BR "mpls label,tc,s,ttl[,...]" .
A probe that gets no answer within its timeout yields
.BR no-reply .
Errors are reported by name
.RB ( invalid-argument ", " probes-exhausted ", " permission-denied ", "
.BR address-in-use ", " address-not-available ", " network-down ", "
.BR host-down ", " no-route-network ", " no-route-host ", "
.BR unexpected-error " errno N)."
A malformed line is answered
.B 0 command-parse-error
and an overlong one
.BR "0 command-buffer-overflow" .
.SH PRIVILEGES
Raw sockets need
.BR CAP_NET_RAW :
.PP
.RS
.B setcap cap_net_raw+ep /usr/bin/mtr-rs-packet
.RE
.PP
Without it,
.B mtr-rs-packet
falls back to unprivileged ICMP and UDP datagram sockets with
.BR IP_RECVERR .
Echo replies and time-exceeded errors are still reported. MPLS labels are
not available on that path, and TCP and SCTP probes cannot see intermediate
hops.
.PP
.B SO_MARK
is set per probe, after the privilege drop, so the drop keeps
.B CAP_NET_ADMIN
when the process was granted it. Nothing else survives.
.BR mtr-rs (8)'s
.B \-M
.RB ( \-\-mark )
therefore needs
.B cap_net_admin
on
.BR mtr-rs-packet ,
and root (or
.BR cap_net_admin )
for the client's own route lookup. Grant both capabilities with:
.PP
.RS
.B setcap cap_net_raw,cap_net_admin+ep /usr/bin/mtr-rs-packet
.RE
.PP
.B "check\-support feature mark"
answers
.B ok
only when the capability is really held.
.PP
.B CAP_NET_ADMIN
is one of the broadest Linux capabilities: interface and routing
configuration, netfilter, promiscuous mode, netlink administration. The drop
runs before the first command is read, so a helper that started with the
capability keeps it for the whole run, on every run and not only when
.B \-M
is used. The same holds under
.BR "sudo mtr-rs" .
Grant it only if you use
.BR \-M .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/mtr.is.run.under.sudo
When this file is present, the
.BR mtr-rs (8)
client ignores
.B $MTR_PACKET
and
.BR $MTR_RS_LOG ,
refuses
.BR \-F ", " \-\-config " and " \-\-init-config ,
and does not read the default user configuration file.  Those are the
environment- and argument-controlled paths that the client would otherwise
execute, read or create.  The marker keeps a sudoers rule for
.B mtr-rs
from granting arbitrary path access.  It does not drop the client's root
privileges, and it does not restrict the network targets or probe options the
caller may select.  The sudo policy should still sanitise the environment and
grant only the intended command line.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mtr-rs (8)
.SH AUTHORS
mtr-rs is a Rust port of mtr by Matt Kimball, Roger Wolff and others; the
protocol and behaviour follow mtr 0.96.
